# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
BW = [
dict(id="dsa-kernel", g=8, title="Sparse attention, as a kernel", tag="what you are handed, and why it is hard",
 intro="DeepSeek Sparse Attention picks the 2,048 most relevant historical positions with a lightweight indexer, then attends only to those. The indexer is someone else's problem. What lands on the kernel is an arbitrary list of indices — and that is where the difficulty starts.",
 problem="Cutting 100,000 tokens down to 2,048 removes almost all the arithmetic, but the 2,048 survivors are <em>scattered</em> through the KV cache. The kernel needs <code>KV[17]</code>, <code>KV[831]</code>, <code>KV[9201]</code>, <code>KV[45002]</code> — not a slice. Every gather can land on a different HBM address, so nothing streams.",
 solves="Naming this correctly is the whole setup: sparse attention trades a compute problem for a <b>memory-access</b> problem. Once you have said that, every optimisation that follows has an obvious motive.",
 method="The kernel receives Q for T query tokens plus an index list per query. For each tile of indices it <b>gathers</b> the corresponding K and V into shared memory, runs QK on Tensor Cores, updates an online softmax, accumulates PV, and moves on. Gather does not make the source contiguous — it pulls the currently needed subset on chip, and pays the scatter again next tile.",
 rel=[("flashattention","the same tile-and-fuse idea, here over a scattered index list"),("full-vs-linear-attention","a different way to avoid reading every token"),("b200-hardware","the machine this has to run on"),("kv-cache","what the indices point into")],
 trade=[("Fewer FLOPs, worse access pattern","dense attention streams; sparse attention random-accesses, and bandwidth utilisation collapses"),("Correctness is bounded","the competition required matching the BF16 reference within abs_err 0.1, which rules out the cheap low-precision shortcuts"),("The indexer is a separate cost","the kernel is only fast in the context of a pipeline that already paid to choose the indices")],
 say="Sparse attention does not make attention easy — it converts an arithmetic problem into an irregular-memory problem. Everything after that is a response to the scatter."),

dict(id="b200-hardware", g=8, title="The B200, and the one rule", tag="148 SMs, and a block that cannot move",
 intro="You cannot reason about this kernel without a hardware model. The one that matters here is small: a GPU is 148 independent SMs, a block is assigned to exactly one of them, and shared memory belongs to the SM.",
 problem="Performance intuition usually stops at “the GPU is parallel”. That is not enough to explain why a perfectly good kernel ran at 5% utilisation — for that you need to know what the scheduler can and cannot do with the blocks you launch.",
 solves="One fact does most of the work: <b>a CUDA block is assigned to one SM and stays there</b>. Its threads share one shared-memory allocation and one <code>__syncthreads()</code> domain, so the hardware cannot lend half a block to an idle neighbour. Launch 8 blocks on 148 SMs and 140 SMs idle — permanently.",
 method="<b>HBM</b> and <b>L2</b> are GPU-wide. Each <b>SM</b> has warp schedulers, CUDA cores, Tensor Cores, a register file, and up to <b>228 KB of shared memory</b> carved into a private region per resident block. A <b>warp</b> is 32 threads scheduled together; 128 threads is 4 warps, 256 is 8, 512 is 16. More resident warps means more chances that <em>something</em> is ready to issue while another warp waits on memory.",
 rel=[("split-k","the optimisation that exists because a block cannot span SMs"),("cp-async-buffering","shared memory is the resource it spends"),("dsa-kernel","the workload landing on this machine"),("model-parallelism","the same question one level up — where to cut the work")],
 trade=[("Shared memory buys occupancy or buys buffering, not both","more per block means fewer resident blocks per SM"),("Occupancy is not the goal","it is a means to latency hiding; a kernel that is compute-bound gains nothing from more warps"),("The rule is a constraint, not a bug","indivisible blocks are what make shared memory and __syncthreads() cheap in the first place")],
 say="A block never leaves its SM. That single sentence explains why the grid shape is a design decision and not an implementation detail."),

dict(id="wmma-tensor-cores", g=9, title="WMMA and Tensor Cores", tag="v1 · make the arithmetic fast",
 intro="The first version moved QK and PV off the scalar CUDA cores and onto the Tensor Cores through WMMA. It was a real speedup, and it did not touch the actual bottleneck — which is the more interesting half of the story.",
 problem="Attention is two matrix multiplies with a softmax between them. Written as scalar fused multiply-adds, the hardware spends its issue slots and register bandwidth on bookkeeping rather than on arithmetic.",
 solves="Tensor Cores are fixed-function hardware for exactly one shape of work: a small matrix multiply-accumulate. One instruction does a whole tile, so the arithmetic stops being the thing you are paying for.",
 method="<b>WMMA</b> — Warp Matrix Multiply Accumulate — lets one warp cooperatively issue an MMA. Here: <code>m16n16k16</code> tiles in <b>BF16</b>, used for <em>both</em> QK and PV, with <b>FP32 accumulation</b> so the running sums stay stable. Low precision in, higher precision in the accumulator, which is the standard shape.",
 rel=[("b200-hardware","the units this targets"),("cp-async-buffering","the next bottleneck, once compute is fast"),("flashattention","a separate idea often confused with this one"),("dsa-kernel","the operation being accelerated")],
 trade=[("It fixed the wrong bottleneck first","v1 still ran ~600 µs, because the scattered gather was untouched"),("Precision is a budget, not a free lever","matching the BF16 reference within abs_err 0.1 is what ruled out more aggressive quantisation"),("WMMA is not FlashAttention","one maps math onto hardware, the other removes memory round-trips — conflating them is a common interview tell")],
 say="Tensor Cores made the arithmetic fast. That mattered, but the kernel was never arithmetic-bound — which is exactly why v1 was still 600 microseconds."),

dict(id="cp-async-buffering", g=9, title="cp.async and double buffering", tag="v2 · stop waiting for the gather",
 intro="With the arithmetic fast, the kernel spent its time waiting for scattered KV entries to arrive. Double buffering does not move fewer bytes — it stops the Tensor Cores from standing still while the bytes move.",
 problem="One shared-memory buffer forces a strict alternation: load a tile, compute on it, load the next, compute on that. The DMA path and the Tensor Cores take turns, and each is idle while the other works.",
 solves="They are different hardware, so they can run at the same time. Two buffers let the gather for tile B be issued before tile A has been consumed, and the stage cost drops from the sum of the two to roughly the larger of the two.",
 method="<b>cp.async</b> issues an asynchronous global→shared copy that does not block the issuing warp. With <b>buffer A and buffer B</b>, the loop computes on one while filling the other, then swaps. Serially, <code>T = T_load + T_compute</code>; pipelined, <code>T ≈ max(T_load, T_compute)</code>.",
 rel=[("wmma-tensor-cores","the version this builds on"),("b200-hardware","shared memory is the budget being spent"),("split-k","the bottleneck this could not reach"),("flashattention","tiling and fusion, the same family of idea")],
 trade=[("Bounded by the larger side","if the load is 10 and the compute is 2, you hide 2 — 600 µs to 470 µs, not 600 to 300"),("More buffers cost occupancy","each extra stage is more shared memory per block, so fewer blocks stay resident — you buy latency hiding with the occupancy that was doing the hiding"),("It does not reduce traffic at all","if you are actually bandwidth-limited, pipelining is the wrong lever")],
 say="Double buffering hides latency; it does not remove traffic. The 130 microseconds it bought are exactly the amount of load that happened to fit behind the compute."),

dict(id="split-k", g=9, title="Split-K", tag="v3 · the change that actually mattered",
 intro="The benchmark ran eight query tokens on a 148-SM GPU. One block per query means eight blocks, which means eight SMs. No amount of making a block faster fixes a grid that small — you have to change the decomposition.",
 problem="With <code>T = 8</code> and one block per query token, the launch produced <b>8 blocks</b>. A block cannot span SMs, so about <b>5% of the GPU</b> had work and the other 140 SMs sat idle for the whole kernel.",
 solves="Splitting each query's 2,048 entries across <code>S</code> blocks turns 8 blocks into <code>T × S</code> blocks. At <code>S = 32</code> that is 256 blocks — enough independent work that every SM has something to run, and a queue behind it. This was the single largest jump in the project: <b>470 µs → 95 µs</b>.",
 method="Each query's index list is cut into <code>S</code> chunks of <code>2048 / S</code> entries. Each block processes one chunk and writes a <b>partial online-softmax state</b> — <code>(M, LSE, O)</code> — to scratch. A second <b>reduce kernel</b> merges the S partials per query into the final output.",
 rel=[("b200-hardware","the indivisible-block rule that makes this necessary"),("online-softmax-merge","what makes splitting mathematically legal"),("autotune-polish","choosing S, which turns out not to be a formula"),("model-parallelism","the same instinct at cluster scale — re-cut the work to fit the machine")],
 trade=[("It adds work rather than removing it","S partial states per query, scratch traffic to write them, and a whole second kernel to read them"),("Wall-clock still falls","because far more of the machine computes at once — latency and total work are different quantities"),("So S must be tuned, not maximised","past some point the reduction overhead eats the parallelism gain"),("It is not about fitting in shared memory","one block could already tile through 2,048 entries — saying otherwise is the most common misreading of Split-K")],
 say="Split-K is a change of parallel decomposition, not a memory trick. The question it answers is ‘how do I get enough independent blocks to fill 148 SMs’, and the answer costs a reduction."),

dict(id="online-softmax-merge", g=10, title="Merging the splits", tag="the state that makes Split-K legal",
 intro="Once 32 blocks each see a slice of the same query's scores, something has to reassemble one softmax out of them. You cannot normalise the slices independently — and the fix is the same running state FlashAttention uses.",
 problem="Softmax is not additive over a partition. Chunk A with scores <code>[10, 9]</code> and chunk B with scores <code>[1, 0]</code> both normalise to roughly <code>[0.73, 0.27]</code>, even though globally B should contribute almost nothing. Local normalisation destroys exactly the information that says so.",
 solves="Keeping <b>sufficient statistics</b> instead of a normalised answer lets independently-computed chunks be merged into the exact global result — not an approximation.",
 method="Each block emits <code>mᵢ = max(scores)</code>, <code>lᵢ = Σ exp(s − mᵢ)</code> and <code>oᵢ = Σ exp(s − mᵢ)·V</code>. The reduce kernel takes <code>m = max mᵢ</code>, rescales every partial onto it with <code>exp(mᵢ − m)</code>, sums, and divides: <code>O = o / l</code>. Exact, and order-independent.",
 rel=[("split-k","the decomposition this makes possible"),("flashattention","the same state, carried tile to tile instead of block to block"),("self-attention","the softmax being reconstructed"),("autotune-polish","the kernel that runs this, and got faster in v4")],
 trade=[("It is exact, which is the point","an approximate merge would have failed the abs_err 0.1 correctness bar outright"),("But it is not free","S partial states per query must be written to and read from global scratch"),("The reduction scales with S","more splits means more to merge — the other half of why S has an optimum")],
 say="Online softmax is what makes it legal to look at attention scores in pieces — whether the pieces are tiles inside one block, or blocks spread across the GPU."),

dict(id="autotune-polish", g=10, title="Autotuning the split factor", tag="v4 · why a formula could not do it",
 intro="Once the shape of the kernel was right, the remaining question was what value of S to use. v3 guessed with a heuristic. v4 stopped guessing and measured — and the measurements did not agree with the heuristic.",
 problem="v3 chose <code>S ≈ 2·SMs / T</code>, aiming for roughly 256 blocks on the theory that block count is what fills the GPU. But two configurations can produce the same grid and completely different per-block work: <code>T=8, S=32</code> is 64 KV per block; <code>T=32, S=8</code> is 256 KV per block.",
 solves="Benchmarking the candidates picks the configuration that is actually fastest for <em>this</em> workload, and the winner moves between workloads. Together with the warp changes: <b>95 µs → 57 µs</b>.",
 method="Sweep the valid powers of two that divide <code>TOPK / BLOCK_K</code>, benchmark each, keep the fastest. Alongside it, two warp-configuration fixes: the <b>reduce kernel</b> went from 4 warps covering 16 heads in four serial passes to <b>16 warps, one per head, in one pass</b>; the <b>split kernel</b> went from 4 warps to <b>8</b>, so a warp stalled on a scattered read is covered by another.",
 rel=[("split-k","the parameter being tuned"),("online-softmax-merge","what the reduce kernel is computing"),("b200-hardware","warps and occupancy, the thing being traded"),("load-balancing","the same idea — measured behaviour beats a predictive formula")],
 trade=[("Tuning time is real time","a sweep costs compilation and benchmarking, which is fine offline and awkward if shapes change at runtime"),("It is workload-specific by construction","the tuned value is a fact about one shape, not a law"),("It explains, but does not predict","autotuning finds the winner without telling you why — profiling is still what turns a number into understanding")],
 say="Block count alone does not determine performance. Changing S changes memory traffic, execution time, register and shared-memory pressure, and reduction cost all at once — no single formula captures six things, so you measure."),
]
